WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.200
Once I've chosen a pull request,

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:04.980
I want to understand how
its changes fit together.

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:08.420
I'll open Change Stack, walk
through the layers and connect

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:11.200
the invoice search and discount
behavior to the code and tests.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:15.400
Then I'll filter the findings and ask
a question about the relevant code.

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:17.860
We'll finish with the
review options and the

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:20.200
conditions that need to
be met before a merge.

00:00:21.170 --> 00:00:24.190
From the pull request card in
Triage, I open Change Stack.

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:29.050
This is our checkout and invoice change
and overview is the starting point.

00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:32.110
I read the summary to understand
what the feature is trying

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:34.630
to do and then look at the
areas that need attention.

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:37.290
For this application, I'm
checking two promises.

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:40.290
Customers should see their own invoices and

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:42.050
qualifying orders should
receive a discount.

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:44.990
I also check which review
version I'm reading.

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:47.930
The findings belong to
that version of the code.

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:51.790
If the author pushes an update,
I need to inspect the newer

00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:54.410
review before deciding whether
the issue is addressed.

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:58.810
I open the layers to work through
related changes in smaller groups.

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:03.170
The promotion pricing layer shows how
discount eligibility is calculated.

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:06.450
Bulk discount shows where
that behavior is used.

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:09.330
Invoice search and export
cover another part of the

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:12.070
feature with tests and
documentation in their own layer.

00:01:12.730 --> 00:01:15.550
I select the invoice layer and
scroll to the search query.

00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:18.450
The customer supplies search text and the

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:20.530
application uses it to retrieve invoices.

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:24.610
I check how the text and the
customer's identity enter the query.

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:27.400
Next, I look at the discount comparison.

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:30.660
The requirement includes an
order of exactly 10 items

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:33.320
while the strict, greater
than condition excludes it.

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:38.420
That discount discussion is retained
in this demo's review history.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:42.880
I use it to examine the behavior without
presenting it as a newly open finding.

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:45.640
Finally, I look at the related tests.

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:48.500
I want to see whether they
check the behavior we expect

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:51.840
at the boundary as well as
an ordinary successful case.

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:55.300
I open the findings
controls and set a minimum

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:58.060
severity to focus on the
more serious concerns.

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:02.380
Then, I open the security finding
beside the affected query.

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:07.520
The explanation describes how search text
can become part of a database command.

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:12.160
I read it alongside the code and check
what that means for the customer's data.

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:16.310
A useful correction needs to keep the
search within the customer's account,

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:20.640
including when the input contains
characters with special meaning in SQL.

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:24.680
I can also include resolved
and outdated threads to

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:28.120
revisit earlier discussions,
such as the discount example.

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:33.160
Their status matters when I am
deciding what still needs work.

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:37.040
Before finishing the review,
I check the filters again

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:39.420
so I don't overlook another
finding I need to read.

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:41.560
On the discount's inline item,

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:44.420
I select Continue to open a
conversation about that code.

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:49.360
I see what happens when an order
contains 10 items and the minimum is 10.

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:52.700
I also ask about tests and
expirations because a quantity

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:55.660
fix should leave an expired
promotion ineligible.

00:02:56.360 --> 00:02:58.700
The first answer needs clarification.

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:03.580
I restate the rule at least 10 items
and only before the expiration time.

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:07.020
Then, I compare the follow-up
with the condition in the code.

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:09.720
The checks we need are straightforward,

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:13.130
below the minimum, exactly
at it and above it.

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:16.000
At the expiration time, the
promotion should no longer apply.

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:19.280
I ask for a short summary
of those expectations.

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:23.080
That gives me concrete behavior
to verify in a proposed fix.

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:26.740
This conversation belongs to
the review where it started.

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:30.600
If the code changes, I'll open a new
conversation on the updated review.

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:33.720
I open the review options.

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:38.220
From this reviewer account, I can choose
comment, approve or request changes.

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:41.620
The invoice search concern
still needs investigation.

00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:45.000
For this demo, I will
leave the pull request open

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:47.560
and preview the choices
without submitting a review.

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:52.800
In a working review, I request
changes needed to address the concern,

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:55.960
then inspect the updated code
and tests before approving.

00:03:56.480 --> 00:03:58.900
I also check the repository's
merge requirements.

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:02.800
CodeRabbit's assessment informs
the decision while the provider's

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:06.420
branch rules determine which
checks and approvals are enforced.

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:11.620
A request for changes on its own
doesn't prove that a merge is blocked.

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:14.320
I need to confirm the rules
that apply to this branch.

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:17.020
We follow the change through its layers,

00:04:17.460 --> 00:04:20.120
check the findings and
clarify the expected behavior.

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:24.940
We can now explain what needs to
happen before the team accepts it.
